From ec1afaa21e43a7968fe9bb55bdf2e978b6c6b25f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thierry Laurion <insurgo@riseup.net>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:03:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] soc/intel/common/block/gpio: lock the PLTRST pad on all
 configurations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Origin: Dasharo/coreboot `dasharo` branch commit 247af1ec5289fd9925c63ccd6c8a69a353197f0b (2026-09-08, direct push), modified
Upstream-Status: Pending — Gerrit mirror is review.coreboot.org topic tpm_gpio_pltrst_lock (CB:95509-95516; of which 95509/95510/95511 merged to coreboot main as of 2026-10-10); supersedes Dasharo/coreboot PR #959 (closed 2026-09-15, not merged)
Refs: "TPM GPIO fail" https://mkukri.xyz/2024-06-01/tpm-gpio-fail.html ; coreboot ticket #576 ; https://github.com/tlaurion/tpm-gpio-fail

The option gated itself on whoever was going to issue APM_CNT_FINALIZE:
coreboot under INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN, or the payload under
SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM. Both work, but relying on the payload
leaves the pad writable for as long as the payload runs, and ties the
mitigation to whether the payload chose to lock the flash: in heads, for
instance, turning off write protection also skips the finalize SMI, and
with it the pad lock.

The SoC now issues its own APM_CNT_GPIO_LOCK SMI from the ramstage, so
neither finalize trigger is needed, and the dependency goes. The pad is
locked before the payload is entered, on every configuration, whether or
not anything ever issues APM_CNT_FINALIZE.

Signed-off-by: Filip Lewiński <filip.lewinski@3mdeb.com>
Signed-off-by: Thierry Laurion <insurgo@riseup.net>
---
 src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig | 26 +++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
index 7f4c2edc00..0073dbfbde 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
@@ -65,7 +65,6 @@ config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_PLTRST_PAD
 	default y
 	depends on SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_HAS_PLTRST_PAD
 	depends on HAVE_SMI_HANDLER
-	depends on INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN || SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM
 	depends on SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_USING_SBI || \
 		   SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_USING_PCR
 	select SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_SMM_LOCK_GPIO_PADS
@@ -77,17 +76,20 @@ config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_PLTRST_PAD
 	  without restarting the measured boot chain and can be replayed with
 	  arbitrary values.
 
-	  Lock the pad configuration from the SMM finalize handler, i.e. after
-	  FSP has run its notify phases, so the pad mode can no longer be
-	  changed. Only the configuration is locked. The Tx state is left
-	  writable, as it has no effect while the pad drives PLTRST#.
-
-	  The finalize handler runs when APM_CNT_FINALIZE is issued. coreboot
-	  issues it on the normal boot path when INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN is set,
-	  and always on S3 resume, where the payload does not run. When
-	  SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM defers the SPI and LPC lockdown to
-	  the payload, the payload issues it instead, so the pad configuration
-	  stays writable for as long as the payload runs.
+	  Lock the pad configuration from SMM at the end of the ramstage, i.e.
+	  after FSP has run its notify phases and unlocked every pad, so the
+	  pad mode can no longer be changed. Only the configuration is
+	  locked. The Tx state is left writable, as it has no effect while
+	  the pad drives PLTRST#.
+
+	  The SoC applies the lock from its ramstage, which runs with SMM
+	  privilege on the SoCs that support this, before handing control to
+	  the payload. It does not depend on INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN, on the
+	  payload, or on APM_CNT_FINALIZE being issued at all, so the pad is
+	  already locked by the time anything after the firmware can run. When
+	  the finalize SMI is issued anyway, the SMM finalize handler locks the
+	  same pad again, which is harmless: locking an already locked pad is a
+	  write of a bit that is already set.
 
 	  Say N on a board that does not use this pad for PLTRST#, or that uses
 	  it as a GPIO: locking the configuration of a pad that already is in
-- 
2.47.3

